===Noun===
{{en-noun|reindeer|s}}

# {{lb|en|plural: '''reindeer'''}} Any [[Arctic]] and [[subarctic]]-[[dwell]]ing [[deer]] of the [[species]] {{taxfmt|Rangifer tarandus|species}}, with a number of [[subspecies]].
#: {{ux|en|Santa Claus' sleigh is supposedly pulled by eight '''reindeer'''}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=D[aniel] Fenning| chapter=LAPLAND| chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=pkJgAAAAcAAJ&pg=PT589| title=The Royal English Dictionary; or, A Treasury of the English Language| edition=3rd improved| location=London| publisher=Printed for R. Baldwin, Hawes and Co., T. Caslon, S. Crowder, J. Johnson, Wilson and Fell, Robinson and Roberts, and B. Collins| year=1768| oclc=22419759| passage=Here is a prodigious number of wild beaſts, as ſtags, bears, wolves, foxes of various colours, martens, hares, glittens, beavers, otters, elk, and '''rein deer''': the latter is leſs than a stag.}}
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1832
|first=Charles
|last=Lyell
|authorlink=Charles Lyell
|chapter=VI
|title=[[w:Principles of Geology|Principles of Geology: Being an Attempt to Explain the Former Changes of the Earth's Surface, by Reference to Causes Now in Operation]]
|volume=II
|volumeurl=https://archive.org/details/PrinciplesgeoloVol2Lyel/
|location=London
|publisher=[[w:John Murray (publishing house)|John Murray]]
|OCLC=934484843
|page=94
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/PrinciplesgeoloVol2Lyel/page/94/
|text=The '''rein-deer''', which in Scandinavia can scarcely exist to the south of the sixty-fifth parallel, descends, in consequence of the greater coldness of the climate, to the fiftieth degree, in Chinese Tartary, and often roves into a country of more southern latitude than any part of England.}}
#* {{quote-journal| 1=en| author=w:Nancy Langston| title=Mining the Boreal North| url=http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/pub/2013/2/mining-the-boreal-north| archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20160413161452/http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/pub/2013/2/mining-the-boreal-north| archivedate=13 April 2016| magazine=w:American Scientist| month=March| year=2013| volume=101| issue=2| page=98| passage='''Reindeer''' are well suited to the taiga’s frigid winters. They can maintain a thermogradient between body core and the environment of up to 100 degrees, in part because of insulation provided by their fur, and in part because of counter-current vascular heat exchange systems in their legs and nasal passages.| accessdate=10 May 2013}}
